From: Fabien Danieau <fabien.danieau@pollen-robotics.com>
Subject: [PATCH] webrtcsink: add mpph264enc encoder support

Add support for Rockchip's MPP hardware H.264 encoder, so that webrtcsink can
drive it the way it drives every other encoder it knows: pick it without putting
a software converter in front, and let congestion control move its bitrate.

Modelled on `webrtcsink: add v4l2h264enc encoder support`, and touching the same
five places -- the converter chain, configure_encoder, is_bitrate_supported, and
the bitrate getter and setter. An encoder present in only some of them is worse
than one in none: without the last three webrtcsink logs "Bitrate handling is
not supported yet for mpph264enc" at every session and the link never adapts,
which on wifi means dropped frames instead of lower quality.

mpph264enc differs from the V4L2 encoder in two ways worth stating. It converts
on the SoC's 2D engine (RGA) and accepts a dozen raw formats on its sink pad, so
the right converter is none at all rather than a better one. And its bitrate
property is `bps` rather than `bitrate`, in bits per second, with bps-min and
bps-max derived by MPP when left at zero.

Level handling is deliberately absent here, for the reason it is absent for
v4l2h264enc: the encoder is the only thing that knows the negotiated resolution
and frame rate, so it calculates its own level. mpph264enc gained that in a
companion patch to gstreamer-rockchip; before it, the encoder advertised a fixed
level 4.0 (profile-level-id 42c028) while browsers offer 3.1 (42e01f) for 720p30,
and no video reached Firefox or Chrome at all.

Upstream-Status: Pending

--- a/net/webrtc/src/webrtcsink/imp.rs
+++ b/net/webrtc/src/webrtcsink/imp.rs
@@ -684,6 +684,25 @@
 
                 ret.add_many([&vapostproc])?;
                 (vapostproc.clone(), vapostproc)
+            } else if codec
+                .encoder_factory()
+                .is_some_and(|factory| factory.name() == "mpph264enc")
+            {
+                // Rockchip's MPP encoder takes NV12, I420, YUY2, UYVY and a dozen more
+                // formats on its sink pad and performs any conversion it needs on the
+                // SoC's 2D engine (RGA). So unlike the NVIDIA and VA cases above, what
+                // it wants is not a better converter but none at all: a software
+                // `videoconvert ! videoscale` in front of it is a full CPU pass over
+                // every frame, which on the four Cortex-A55s of an RK3566 is precisely
+                // the cost the hardware encoder exists to avoid.
+                //
+                // The trade-off, stated plainly: without `videoscale` this bin cannot
+                // resize, so the negotiated resolution has to be one the source already
+                // produces.
+                let identity = make_element("identity", None)?;
+
+                ret.add_many([&identity])?;
+                (identity.clone(), identity)
             } else {
                 let convert = make_element("videoconvert", None)?;
                 let scale = make_element("videoscale", None)?;
@@ -816,6 +835,14 @@
                 enc.set_property("key-int-max", 256u32);
                 enc.set_property_from_str("rate-control", "cbr");
             }
+            "mpph264enc" => {
+                // `bps` is the target; MPP derives bps-min/bps-max from it when they
+                // are left at 0. `gop` is in frames and -1 means "one per second",
+                // which would put a keyframe on the wire every second for no reason:
+                // webrtcsink asks for one via PLI when a consumer actually needs it.
+                enc.set_property("bps", start_bitrate);
+                enc.set_property("gop", 2560i32);
+            }
             "nvv4l2h264enc" => {
                 enc.set_property("bitrate", start_bitrate);
                 if enc.has_property("preset-level") {
@@ -1170,6 +1197,7 @@
                 | "vaapivp8enc"
                 | "vaapivp9enc"
                 | "qsvh264enc"
+                | "mpph264enc"
                 | "nvv4l2h264enc"
                 | "nvv4l2vp8enc"
                 | "nvv4l2vp9enc"
@@ -1201,6 +1229,7 @@
             | "vah265enc" | "vah265lpenc" | "vaav1enc" | "vaav1lpenc" => {
                 (self.element.property::<u32>("bitrate") * 1000) as i32
             }
+            "mpph264enc" => self.element.property::<u32>("bps") as i32,
             "openh264enc" | "nvv4l2h264enc" | "nvv4l2vp8enc" | "nvv4l2vp9enc" | "rav1enc"
             | "nvv4l2av1enc" => (self.element.property::<u32>("bitrate")) as i32,
             _ => return Err(WebRTCSinkError::BitrateNotSupported),
@@ -1244,6 +1273,7 @@
             "openh264enc" | "nvv4l2h264enc" | "nvv4l2vp8enc" | "nvv4l2vp9enc" | "nvv4l2av1enc" => {
                 self.element.set_property("bitrate", bitrate as u32)
             }
+            "mpph264enc" => self.element.set_property("bps", bitrate as u32),
             "rav1enc" => self.element.set_property("bitrate", bitrate),
             _ => return Err(WebRTCSinkError::BitrateNotSupported),
         }
